  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 PM EST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\n- Cold frontal rain expected Sunday into Sunday evening\n\nThe dry weather period continues across Southwest Lower Michigan\ntonight into Saturday. We have not had measurable precipitation \nat Grand Rapids in about a week. \n\nRain returns to the forecast for Sunday into Sunday evening\nalong and ahead of a cold front. The front will be driven through\nthe area by upper shortwaves that will eventually phase over the\nplains. Currently the shortwaves can be seen 1) spinning south over\nCalifornia and 2) moving through Western Canada. Forcing will \noccur through lift along the frontal zone and be aided by the \nright entrance region of the upper jet sliding through the Great \nLakes. Precipitation will be light, less than a quarter of an inch\nin all areas. Many areas will likely see less than a tenth of an \ninch. The reason precipitation will be on the low side is PWAT \nvalues only briefly reach to around 1 inch as well as the speed of\nthe front. It's progressive nature will lead to a limited period \nof rain, about 8 hours or so. The length of time the deeper \nmoisture is present factors in here as well. \n\n- Lake effect snow possible Monday and Monday night\n\nThere is a chance for lake effect snow during this time frame but\nanything that develops will be very light. Moisture profiles\nsuggest it make be tough to produce much more than a few flurries.\nThe 1000-850mb layer RH is so low that it may be hard to produce\nlake effect despite significant instability. We have delta T's\naround 20C Monday night, but the 1000-850mb layer RH is barely\nflirting with 70 percent which is usually a needed ingredient. \nBottom line we have 20 pops in the forecast for some light snow \nshowers. No accumulation is expected at this time. \n\n- Model disparity remains for Holiday period next week\n\nThe evolution of the pattern in the Wednesday night through\nThursday night time frame remains uncertain which leads to a lower\nconfidence forecast. The ECMWF has a more formidable upper wave\nmoving through the Great Lakes while the GFS has the stronger wave\nmuch further south across the deep south of the U.S. At the\nsurface ridging is in place over our area which leads us to think\nthe forecast will likely trend drier with time. The main surface\nlow towards the end of next week will be off the eastern seaboard\nwhich supports drier weather as well for us. We have 20-30 pops in\nthe forecast during the holiday travel period. As of now, we are\nnot expecting significant weather during this time frame. We will\nbe monitoring the trends in the upper trough progression as well\nas precipitation totals. GFS and ECWMF ensembles still show\nprecipitation in our area, but it seems to be trending down.\nAgain, low pops for some light rain/snow showers is all we are\ncarrying in the forecast at this time.\n\n- One more day of mild temperatures then back to reality\n\nAnother day of temperatures pushing into the 60s on Saturday will\ncontinue our above normal streak another day. Normal highs are in\nthe lower 40s. Sunday will be a transition day as a cold front\nmoves through and temperatures begin to fall. High temperatures \nwill be in the upper 30s to lower 40s Monday through Thanksgiving\nwhich is very close to normal.",
